pastex (1.10.11-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Paste even when the window manager refuses to minimize. The 1.10.10 log
    shows Muffin ignoring every minimize request it was sent: the window stayed
    focused for the whole 900 ms budget (focus=ours, tk=normal), Ctrl+V was
    never sent, and nothing was pasted at all. Waiting longer can never help a
    request that is being dropped, so after 200 ms the click now hands the
    focus to the application behind Pastex -- the topmost mapped window below
    it in _NET_CLIENT_LIST_STACKING -- with xdotool windowfocus, a plain
    XSetInputFocus that no window manager can refuse -- and raises it. The text
    then goes where the click meant it to go, the window is never minimized,
    and after the paste the focus returns to Pastex if it is still on that
    window.
  * Log the outcome of the minimize request (minimized / did not minimize, with
    its duration) so a refusing window manager is visible in error.log instead
    of looking like a mystery.

 -- Patrick Buhagiar <patrickbuhagiar24@gmail.com>  Thu, 19 Nov 2026 02:10:00 +0700

pastex (1.10.10-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Remove the last two-second stall in a click. The window is now minimized
    through the window manager, so Tk's iconify() must never run on that click
    again: the "still in the foreground" retry called it anyway and paid the
    same two-second wait Tk spends when the window manager does not report the
    state change. In the 1.10.9 log the copy finished at +18 ms but the paste
    was only sent at +2226 ms because of exactly that call.
  * Bring the window back without waiting for the focus. xdotool
    windowactivate --sync waits for the window to become active, and Muffin's
    focus-stealing protection refuses an activation that carries no timestamp:
    the request burned its two-second timeout and reported failure even though
    the window manager was fine. Restoring is now windowmap plus windowraise
    and windowactivate without --sync, verified through _NET_WM_STATE for about
    half a second, and only a real refusal hands the job back to Tk's
    deiconify() retry.

 -- Patrick Buhagiar <patrickbuhagiar24@gmail.com>  Wed, 18 Nov 2026 23:40:00 +0700

pastex (1.10.9-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Stop hiding and restoring the window through Tk. Tk's iconify() and
    deiconify() wait up to two seconds for the window manager to report the
    state change, and Muffin never reported it, so every click paid for two
    such waits: the 1.10.8 log shows the copy starting at +2026 ms and the
    paste being sent at +4227 ms while the work itself took 87 ms. The window
    manager is now asked directly -- xdotool windowminimize --sync to hide and
    xdotool windowactivate --sync to bring the window back, both from a worker
    thread -- and Tk is only the fallback when there is no xdotool or no X11
    window id. The restore is verified through the window manager and logged.
  * The window therefore also comes back as soon as the paste is done instead
    of waiting behind a two-second block in the GUI thread.

 -- Patrick Buhagiar <patrickbuhagiar24@gmail.com>  Wed, 18 Nov 2026 22:30:00 +0700

pastex (1.10.8-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Stop re-checking a clipboard that already proved unusable. The 1.10.7 log
    showed the click taking seconds while the work itself was quick: the check
    costs a round trip through the GUI thread plus two clipboard reads. Once a
    click has proved that this machine's clipboard cannot be read back, later
    clicks type the snippet straight away.
  * Time every paste log line from the click. Each line now ends with the
    offset (+180 ms), so a slow click can be read from the log instead of
    guessed at, and a tree refresh that takes 300 ms or more is reported too,
    because a slow repaint delays the click handler behind it.
  * Do not cut a typed snippet short. Typing a long snippet takes as long as it
    takes and the call has its own timeout, so the paste deadline no longer
    fires mid-sentence; the timeout now grows with the text instead of being
    fixed at eight seconds for anything longer than a line.
  * Do not attempt the Pages deployment from a branch. The github-pages
    environment only accepts deployments from main, so a branch build now
    stops after building instead of failing with a protection-rule error.

