pastex (1.10.5-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Verify the copy before pasting. xclip, xsel and wl-copy exit 0 once they
    have handed the text to the process they fork, so a copy whose input was
    lost still reports success and then serves an empty clipboard: the log of
    the Cinnamon session showed "copy: ok=True tool=xclip (9 ms)" while
    pastex --check read the clipboard back as empty. Ctrl+V would then paste
    nothing at all. The clipboard is now read back (a second time, because a
    fresh owner can answer late) before Ctrl+V is sent.
  * Claim the selection with Tk when the tool lost it. If the clipboard does
    not hold the text, Pastex sets it through Tk itself -- no subprocess can
    lose that -- verifies again, and only then sends Ctrl+V.
  * Say so instead of pasting nothing. When no clipboard holds the text the
    paste is cancelled, the window is restored and the status reports that
    nothing was pasted, instead of the "the text is on the clipboard" hint.
  * pastex --check explains an empty read-back the same way.

