pastex (1.10.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Stop configuring the Wayland helpers when the package is installed. Debian
    postinst used to create the 'pastex' group, add the invoking account to it
    (which needs a logout), reload the udev rules and enable a per-user
    ydotoold unit on every install, even though Linux Mint's default X11
    session needs none of it. It now only prints a one-time note pointing at
    `pastex --enable-permissions`, and the executing account is never touched.
  * Ship the optional Wayland payload as inert templates. The udev rule and the
    ydotoold user unit moved from /usr/lib/udev/rules.d and /usr/lib/systemd/
    user to /usr/lib/pastex; only `pastex --enable-permissions` (the existing
    pkexec helper) copies the rule into place, adds the group membership and
    enables the unit. A package upgrade therefore cannot change device
    permissions by itself.
  * Report the X11 session as needing no permissions anywhere in the UI. The
    pastex group and the /dev/uinput rows, the "Next steps" advice and the
    CLI hint no longer appear in `--check`, `--permissions` or the paste
    failure dialog on X11; they are replaced by a single line stating that
    xdotool types through XTEST. The paste failure dialog also stops blaming
    /dev/uinput there and points at focus instead.
  * Move the Wayland-only tool recommendations. Recommends is now xclip, xsel
    and xdotool, so `apt install pastex` on X11 no longer drags in wl-clipboard,
    ydotool and python3-dbus; those three are Suggests. A test keeps every
    clipboard tool the code calls declared in one of the two fields.
  * Fix the copy failure message: it no longer mentions the internal clipboard
    fallback that was removed in 1.4.0, and it names the Mint packages
    (xclip/xsel/xdotool) instead of wl-clipboard only.
  * Five new mutation checks keep the X11 behaviour from regressing.

 -- Patrick Buhagiar <patrickbuhagiar24@users.noreply.github.com>  Mon, 16 Nov 2026 14:00:00 +0000

pastex (1.9.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Target Linux Mint only. Pastex now documents, packages and tests for Linux
    Mint (Cinnamon, MATE and Xfce; X11 by default and the Cinnamon Wayland
    session) instead of Ubuntu with a GNOME-first PPA. The Ubuntu PPA
    workflow and the apt-repo/pastex.list.example file are gone; the only
    supported installation is APT from the signed archive.
  * Rename the APT archive suite from "stonking" to "mint". The suite is a
    fixed label for this project, not a Mint release codename, so the same
    architecture-independent package serves every Mint edition and release.
    A sources file written by 1.8.x still names "stonking"; re-running the
    README installation block, or replacing that suite name with "mint", is
    enough to keep upgrading (the keyring does not change).
  * Rewrite the README installation section to match the youtube repository:
    one subshell that downloads the keyring into a temporary directory, prints
    the 40-character fingerprint for confirmation, writes the deb822
    .sources file with Signed-By, and then documents upgrade, removal and
    local package installation, including the expected
    "Hit: ... mint InRelease" line.
  * Say the default session first: on X11 automatic paste needs only xclip and
    xdotool, with no /dev/uinput, no pastex group and permission checks that
    are reported as not needed. The Wayland clipboard owner
    (ext-data-control-v1) is described as applying only to compositors that
    implement it, and panel wording replaces dock wording throughout.
  * Translate every remaining Indonesian string into English: the startup line
    is now "[paste] ready: copy=... paste=... ydotoold=running|not-running",
    the clipboard abort message reads "aborted: the window was already
    restored", and the paste log lines, code comments and test messages are
    English.

